# Summary
Introduced a denial-of-service risk: POST Object requests are now streamed without the previous body size limit, and the parser buffers non-file fields without any cap. This enables unbounded memory growth for large or malformed multipart fields.
The commit routes all POST Object requests to a new streaming handler before the normal body collection (which enforces MAX_BODY_SIZE). The streaming multipart parser accumulates non-file field data in an internal buffer until a boundary is found, with no size checks. An unauthenticated client can send a multipart request with a huge field (or omit the next boundary) and continuously stream data, causing the buffer to grow unbounded and potentially crash the server or exhaust memory.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm streaming POST Object path bypasses MAX_BODY_SIZE body collection (serve.rs:287-325; request.rs:4-6).
- [x] Confirm multipart parser buffers non-file field data without a size cap when no boundary is seen (serve.rs:520-608).
- [x] Confirm non-file fields are stored as full strings with no limit (serve.rs:582-590, 702-715).
- [x] Confirm authentication happens after form-field parsing, allowing unauthenticated buffering (http/mod.rs:1746-1784).
- [ ] Achieve dynamic reproduction (blocked: build fails due to missing libisal; valgrind/gdb not available).
## Report
Attempted dynamic reproduction first: `cargo build -p argmin-s3` fails because the `libisal` system library is missing (see build_fail.log in artifacts), so the server binary cannot be built to run a crash test. Valgrind and gdb are not installed in the container (`valgrind --version` / `gdb --version` both not found), so valgrind/debugger reproduction was not possible. Code review shows the issue: the request handler routes POST Object requests to `handle_streaming_post_object` before the non-streaming `collect_body` call, so the MAX_BODY_SIZE cap used for normal requests is bypassed for POST Object streaming (serve.rs:287-325; MAX_BODY_SIZE in request.rs:4-6). In the streaming multipart parser, `feed` unconditionally appends incoming bytes to `self.buf`, and for non-file parts it only drains the buffer when a delimiter is found; if the boundary is missing or delayed, the buffer grows without any size check (serve.rs:520-608). When a non-file field is eventually parsed, the entire field value is converted to a `String` and stored in the `fields` vector without any size cap (serve.rs:582-590, 702-715). Authentication for POST Object relies on these form fields and occurs after field parsing (`prepare_streaming_post_object` uses form fields to authenticate; http/mod.rs:1746-1784), so an unauthenticated client can stream an unbounded non-file field and force memory growth before any auth rejection. This matches the reported DoS risk.

