# Attack-path analysis
Final: medium | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: medium | Policy adjusted: medium
## Rationale
The flaw is a real, unauthenticated memory-exhaustion DoS in a core HTTP path, but it impacts availability of a single service instance without demonstrated data compromise or privilege escalation. Default config binds to localhost, and exposure depends on deployment settings, so downgrading from high to medium better reflects probability × impact.
## Likelihood
high - If the HTTP listener is exposed, an unauthenticated client can send a large or malformed multipart field to trigger the issue without special privileges. | Remote network vector
## Impact
medium - Unbounded buffering of multipart fields can exhaust memory and crash or stall the service instance, causing availability loss.
## Assumptions
- argmin-s3 is deployed with ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR bound to an interface reachable by the attacker (e.g., 0.0.0.0 or behind a proxy).
- No upstream reverse proxy enforces a stricter body size limit on POST multipart requests.
- Attackers can send unauthenticated POST Object multipart requests to the HTTP endpoint.
- Network access to the argmin-s3 HTTP listener
- Ability to send multipart/form-data POST Object requests
## Path
n1 -> n2 -> n3 -> n4
## Path evidence
- `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:287-325` - POST Object requests are routed to handle_streaming_post_object before collect_body(), bypassing the standard body size limit.
- `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:517-590` - PostMultipartParser::feed appends to self.buf and buffers non-file fields into memory without size checks.
- `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:676-735` - Multipart fields are accumulated into the fields vector and only later used to prepare the streaming POST context.
- `crates/server-http/src/http/mod.rs:1746-1783` - prepare_streaming_post_object authenticates using form_fields, confirming auth occurs after field parsing.
- `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:1674-1699` - collect_body_with_limit enforces MAX_BODY_SIZE for non-streaming requests, which is skipped by the streaming POST path.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/config.rs:20-43` - Server listens on ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR (default 127.0.0.1:9000), indicating exposure depends on deployment config.
## Narrative
The HTTP handler routes POST Object requests to handle_streaming_post_object before the standard collect_body path that enforces MAX_BODY_SIZE. The streaming multipart parser appends all incoming data to an in-memory buffer and only drains it when a boundary is found; for non-file fields it converts the full buffered content to a String with no size cap. Authentication for POST Object relies on these form fields and occurs after they are parsed. As a result, an unauthenticated client can stream an oversized or boundary-less non-file field and drive unbounded memory growth, leading to a denial-of-service if the endpoint is reachable.
## Controls
- request semaphore limits inflight requests
- body idle timeout on frame reads
- MAX_BODY_SIZE enforcement for non-streaming bodies
- max_connections configuration
## Blindspots
- No runtime PoC due to build dependency constraints (libisal missing).
- No deployment manifests to confirm external exposure or upstream body-size limits.
- Actual operational rate limiting or proxy buffering controls are unknown.

## Reviewer note
Valid. Fixed in commit `6d78835` (`server-http: bound streaming post form fields`).

The shipped fix is narrower and more AWS-aligned than the draft patch in this report. The streaming multipart parser now rejects oversized part headers, enforces field-specific size limits on non-file parts while they are still buffered waiting for the next boundary, and keeps the delimiter-split handling needed for valid streaming uploads. The streaming POST handler also tracks aggregate non-file form bytes and applies the existing 2 KiB `x-amz-meta-*` budget before authentication, so an unauthenticated client can no longer force unbounded pre-auth memory growth with one huge control field or many individually valid control fields.

Regression coverage was added in `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs` for oversized multipart headers, oversized undelimited non-file fields, metadata budget overflow, and aggregate non-file form budget overflow.
